/*!
    \group qtros2-examples
    \title Qt ROS2 Examples
    \brief Example applications that demonstrate the Qt ROS2 Bridge.

    These examples show how to build Qt/QML applications on top of ROS 2 using
    the Qt ROS2 Bridge — from a single publisher in a dozen lines of QML to a
    full robot-monitoring dashboard and a 6-DOF teach pendant. Each one is a
    self-contained Qt application; the ROS 2 side (topics, services, actions,
    and TF) is spoken entirely through the generated QtRos2 QML modules.

    The examples live in the \c examples/ directory of the repository and are
    excluded from the default workspace build via a \c COLCON_IGNORE marker, so
    they do not slow down a module build. Open an example's \c CMakeLists.txt in
    Qt Creator (from a shell that has sourced your ROS 2 environment) to build
    and run it.

    \section1 Before You Start

    All of the examples need a running ROS 2 environment. Source it in every
    terminal you use — the one that launches Qt Creator or the application, and
    any terminal running a ROS 2 backend such as \c turtlesim_node or a
    simulation:

    \badcode
    source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
    \endcode

    \note Before running an example, make sure every ROS 2 node from a previous
    example has been terminated — both the Qt application and any backend nodes
    (\c turtlesim_node, simulation launches, controllers). Two nodes that claim
    the same node name, topics, or TF frames will interfere with each other and
    produce confusing results such as jumping poses or missing data.

    \section1 The Examples

    The list below is ordered roughly from simplest to most complete. If you are
    new to the bridge, start with \l{Simple Publisher} and \l{Simple Subscriber},
    which introduce the \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{Node} type and a single
    publisher/subscriber, continue with \l{Simple Service} and
    \l{Simple Service Client}, which show both ends of a ROS 2 service, then
    move on to the \l{TurtleSim Controller}, which adds actions.

    \annotatedlist qtros2-examples

    \section1 Where To Go Next

    \list
        \li For the message types the examples publish and subscribe to, see the
            \l{Qt ROS2 QML Types} reference.
        \li For guidance on picking sensor message types, see
            \l{Choosing Sensor Message Types}.
        \li For sending velocity commands and navigation goals, see
            \l{Teleoperation and Remote Control}.
    \endlist
*/
